lsewhere, so $C=I_2$. Both matrices are $2 \\times 2$. Row-column multiplication gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(CG)_{11}&=1\\cdot(a_{11})+0\\cdot(a_{21})=a_{11} \\\\ (CG)_{12}&=1\\cdot(a_{12})+0\\cdot(a_{22})=a_{12} \\\\ (CG)_{21}&=0\\cdot(a_{11})+1\\cdot(a_{21})=a_{21} \\\\ (CG)_{22}&=0\\cdot(a_{12})+1\\cdot(a_{22})=a_{22}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nCG=\\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22}\\end{bmatrix}=G.\n$$\n\nFor row $1$ and column $2$, the calculation is $1\\cdot(a_{12})+0\\cdot(a_{22})=a_{12}$. The zero factors remove the other summands, not the off-diagonal entries of the product. A true calculation of another entry does not justify the requested entry. Distinct entries are arbitrary independent real numbers, so the crossed equations need not hold."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186719334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5545ddd0-f8ca-5343-853a-fe316f2ad3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$MC=M$; the requested entry is $(a_{11})\\cdot0+(a_{12})\\cdot1=a_{12}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719326} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8bdd2b-4df8-58bc-8254-8388eeca3711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$MC=M$; the requested entry is $(a_{21})\\cdot0+(a_{22})\\cdot1=a_{22}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719331} {:answer/id #uuid "8329049c-ea92-5dec-84a6-9c823c0b381b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$MC=M$; the requested entry is $(a_{21})\\cdot1+(a_{22})\\cdot0=a_{22}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719333} {:answer/id #uuid "8e426268-bc70-52d1-b924-834ea17ed318" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$MC=M$; the requested entry is $(a_{21})\\cdot0+(a_{22})\\cdot1=a_{21}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186719335} {:answer/id #uuid "cea028d8-a4c9-5951-b812-ad4433a8f2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :a